<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northwest Indian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Little Turtle's War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northwest Indian War was fought from 1786 to 1795 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and a confederation of Native American nations of the Northwest Territory, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, Miami and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>. The confederacy destroyed American armies under Harmar (1790) and St. Clair (1791), but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-wayne">Anthony Wayne</a> at Fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timbers</a> in 1794; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-greenville">Treaty of Greenville</a> (1795) ceded most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northwest-indian-war.jpg" alt="Northwest Indian War" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Greenville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> ceded the lands north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, though the Native peoples living there had no part in it. As American settlers moved in, a Wyandot-led confederacy formed in 1785 to resist them; its members called themselves the United Indian Nations and are better known as the Northwestern Confederacy. In 1786 the Kentucky militia invaded Native land: George Rogers Clark's march along the Wabash achieved nothing, but Benjamin Logan's men burned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> towns on the Mad River, and the elderly chief Moluntha was executed. The confederacy then declared the recent treaties invalid because they had not been made with all its members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Once the Constitution took effect, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> directed the army to enforce American sovereignty. In autumn 1790 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-harmar">Josiah Harmar</a> led 1,100 militiamen and 320 regulars from Fort Washington against Kekionga, the Miami capital, and was defeated. The Natives followed up with raids such as the attack on Big Bottom on 2 January 1791, which killed eleven settlers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair">Arthur St. Clair</a>, governor of the Northwest Territory, then led a second expedition, whose defeat in 1791 destroyed most of the United States Army.</p><p>In March 1792 Congress enlarged the army to more than 5,000 men under Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-wayne">Anthony Wayne</a>, who reorganised it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and drilled it hard, flogging offenders and hanging deserters. Peace envoys sent to the Natives in 1792 were killed, and talks in 1793 failed. In 1794 Lord Dorchester told an Iroquois delegation that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> might soon be at war with the United States, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe">John Graves Simcoe</a> built Fort Miami on the Maumee. Wayne advanced up the Great Miami and Maumee valleys and won a decisive victory at Fallen Timbers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Jay Treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> required its withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> forts, which it left in 1796. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-greenville">Treaty of Greenville</a> the confederacy also gave up tracts around forts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois-country">Illinois Country</a>, recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> rather than Britain as the ruling power and surrendered ten chiefs as hostages until all prisoners were returned. Settlement and removals followed into the 1830s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-turtle">Little Turtle</a> lamented that more of his people had died since Greenville than in the war, and contributors to the Journal of Genocide Research describe the campaigns as attempted genocide, citing Henry Knox's orders to extirpate the Natives he called banditti.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northwest Indian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It ended the <b>Northwest Indian War</b> and limited Indian country to northwestern Ohio.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was part of the <b>Northwest Indian War</b>, in which Native peoples of the Ohio Country fought to expel the settlers from their land.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
